msgid "**user_oidc:** with ``services.sso.oidc.plugin: user_oidc``, Nextcloud signs in through its own Keycloak client ``services.sso.oidc.clients.user_oidc`` instead of the shared one; that client exists only while the plugin is selected and receives back-channel logout tokens on ``services.sso.oidc.backchannel_logout_path``. ``tasks/addons/user_oidc.yml`` registers or updates the provider ``keycloak`` with ``occ user_oidc:provider`` on every deploy, the client secret through ``--clientsecret-env`` from stdin. The task reports a change when the provider read-back differs; ``user_oidc`` returns no secret, so a rotation of the client secret alone is written but reported ``ok``. The provider takes the user id from ``preferred_username`` with the hashed unique id off, display name, email, quota and groups from the claims, group provisioning on. With LDAP, ``user_oidc`` signs a user in to the existing ``user_ldap`` account, so ``ldap_admin_group`` keeps the administrators. Administrator rights also come from the token in both modes: the own client carries the client role ``admin``, declared in ``services.sso.oidc.client_roles`` and granted at every deploy to the RBAC group ``/roles/web-app-nextcloud/administrator`` (``roles/web-app-keycloak/tasks/update/16_client_role_grants.yml``), and a multivalued “User Client Role” mapper adds that role to the groups claim next to the group paths. Group provisioning makes the claim value ``admin`` the Nextcloud group id, because the hashed unique id is off and no group whitelist is set, so a member of the administrator group joins Nextcloud’s ``admin`` group at sign-in and leaves it at the first sign-in after the membership ends. Group provisioning also removes every other group the claim does not name, so an account in ``admin`` that is no member of the administrator group loses ``admin`` at its next ``user_oidc`` sign-in; for the platform administrator, the deploy’s ``occ group:adduser admin`` (``tasks/02_manager_ops/07_admin_identities.yml``) restores it only at the next deploy."
msgstr ""

msgid "**user_oidc:** with `services.sso.oidc.plugin: user_oidc`, Nextcloud signs in through its own Keycloak client `services.sso.oidc.clients.user_oidc` instead of the shared one; that client exists only while the plugin is selected and receives back-channel logout tokens on `services.sso.oidc.backchannel_logout_path`. `tasks/addons/user_oidc.yml` registers or updates the provider `keycloak` with `occ user_oidc:provider` on every deploy, the client secret through `--clientsecret-env` from stdin. The task reports a change when the provider read-back differs; `user_oidc` returns no secret, so a rotation of the client secret alone is written but reported `ok`. The provider takes the user id from `preferred_username` with the hashed unique id off, display name, email, quota and groups from the claims, group provisioning on. With LDAP, `user_oidc` signs a user in to the existing `user_ldap` account, so `ldap_admin_group` keeps the administrators. Administrator rights also come from the token in both modes: the own client carries the client role `admin`, declared in `services.sso.oidc.client_roles` and granted at every deploy to the RBAC group `/roles/web-app-nextcloud/administrator` (`roles/web-app-keycloak/tasks/update/16_client_role_grants.yml`), and a multivalued \"User Client Role\" mapper adds that role to the groups claim next to the group paths. Group provisioning makes the claim value `admin` the Nextcloud group id, because the hashed unique id is off and no group whitelist is set, so a member of the administrator group joins Nextcloud's `admin` group at sign-in and leaves it at the first sign-in after the membership ends. Group provisioning also removes every other group the claim does not name, so an account in `admin` that is no member of the administrator group loses `admin` at its next `user_oidc` sign-in; for the platform administrator, the deploy's `occ group:adduser admin` (`tasks/02_manager_ops/07_admin_identities.yml`) restores it only at the next deploy."
